#{[17592186209860 1029 "In this topic, we'll focus on calculating both simple and conditional probabilities using data from frequency tables."] [17592186209861 1029 "In this topic, we will practice determining probabilities involving one or more characteristics using two-way frequency tables."] [17592186209862 1029 "Questions about unit conversions often appear in real-world contexts and require paying attention to conversion factors.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice converting from larger units of length to smaller ones."] [17592186209863 1029 "Rate problems appear in a variety of contexts (speed, density, cost, and work), often requiring translating a real-world description into a simple division or multiplication of quantities.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying the correct rate, setting up proportional relationships, and applying them to solve problems."] [17592186209864 1029 "Ratio questions show up often in forms like simplifying ratios, reading ratio tables, comparing unit rates, and translating worded situations into proportions.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice expressing, comparing, and applying ratios."] [17592186209865 1029 "Questions involving algebraic expressions with rates or ratios often require translating a real-world rate into symbolic form and manipulating it correctly.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice expressing rates and ratios algebraically, forming equivalent expressions, and setting up equations that describe relationships between variables in real contexts."] [17592186209866 1029 "Density problems often appear in the context of physical quantities (mass, volume) and sometimes in geometric settings. These problems test our ability to use proportional relationships and apply formulas in practical, real-world contexts.\n\nIn this, we’ll practice solving for density, mass, or volume using the definition of density."] [17592186209867 1029 "In the math section of the SAT exam, you may be asked to identify conditions where a linear equation has zero, one, or infinitely many solutions. This concept often appears in questions such as: “If this equation has no solution, what must be true about the constants?”"] [17592186209868 1029 "In the math section of the SAT test, word problems may require one step or multiple steps, sometimes involving operations such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, or rounding.\n\nMake sure to double-check your arithmetic and ensure that your final answer is accurate, uses the correct units, and conforms to the requested format."] [17592186209869 1029 "This topic will provide practice in interpreting the coefficients of linear equations in slope-intercept form."] [17592186209870 1029 "This topic provides practice in evaluating linear functions for specific inputs and combining their values."] [17592186209871 1029 "A common theme on the math section of the SAT is interpreting the meaning of coefficients in linear functions.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice understanding what each term represents in context, such as the $y$-intercept, the unit rate, and constants in both simplified and unsimplified relationships."] [17592186209872 1029 "Often, questions about best-fit lines test the ability to interpret and model data using linear relationships.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying equations of best-fit lines by estimating both the slope and the intercept."] [17592186209873 1029 "Sometimes, questions about data plots may test our ability to read and interpret information from graphs such as scatterplots and line graphs.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice extracting information from visual data, including estimating values, identifying trends, and finding maximum and minimum points."] [17592186209874 1029 "In this topic, we’ll practice recognizing the quadratic and exponential patterns visually and comparing those observations against possible algebraic forms."] [17592186209875 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may see problems involving similar right triangles, where we must recognize that the trigonometric ratios for corresponding angles remain the same.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice applying this principle to connect sine, cosine, and tangent values across similar triangles."] [17592186209876 1029 "We have the quadratic function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. As we've seen, $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nWe'll use this form to compute the gradient of $f$.\n\nTo compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f$, we'll differentiate this expanded form with respect to $x_k$. First, we express our expanded function as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\sum_{i \\neq k} \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{ij} x_i x_j + \\sum_{i \\neq k} A_{ik} x_i x_k + \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{kj} x_k x_j + A_{kk} x_k^2\n$$\n\nThe idea here is to break up the double summation by isolating all the terms that involve the variable $x_k$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first summation is *independent* of $x_k$,\n- the second and third summations are *linear* in $x_k$, and\n- the final term is *quadratic* in $x_k$.\n\nWe can now compute our derivative term by term in a structured way. Differentiating with respect to $x_k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{k}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{k}}[∑_(i \\ne k)∑_(j \\ne k)A_{ij}x_{i}x_{j} + ∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i}x_{k} + ∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{k}x_{j} + A_{kk}x_{k}^{2}] \\\\\n&= ∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i} + ∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{j} + 2A_{kk}x_{k} \\\\\n&= (∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i}) + A_{kk}x_{k} + (∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{j}) + A_{kk}x_{k} \\\\\n&= \\sum _{i = 1}^{n}A_{ik}x_{i} + \\sum _{j = 1}^{n}A_{kj}x_{j} \\\\\n&= (A^{T}x)_{k} + (Ax)_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all\n\n$$\n1\\leq k \\leq n\n$$\n\n, we get the result\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = A^T \\mathbf{x} + A \\mathbf{x} = (A^T + A)\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have the following special cases:\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^T = A$, $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a quadratic form, and our result simplifies to\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = 2 A \\mathbf{x}$.\n- Suppose that $A = I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix. Then, our result for symmetric matrices simplifies to\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{x}) = 2 \\mathbf{x}$\nwhich is a result we saw in a previous lesson."] [17592186209877 1029 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y] = \\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1\n$$\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sqrt3\n$$\n\nlet's compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$.\n\nThis is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}[\\textrm{E}[X \\mid Y]]\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\textrm{E}\\left[\\sqrt{3}\\,Y + 1\\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[\\sqrt{3}Y + 1] \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot E[Y] + E[1] \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186209878 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice evaluating expressions by manipulating and transforming other expressions or equations."] [17592186209879 1029 "This topic will provide you with an opportunity to practice identifying and analyzing solutions to two-variable linear inequalities. We'll determine valid solutions from equations, tables, and diagrams, and identify the inequalities that correspond to given graphical representations."] [17592186209880 1029 "A common theme on the math section of the SAT is modeling real-world situations with inequalities.\n\nTo prepare for this, we'll go over constructing one-variable linear inequalities to represent situations involving quantities and averages. In some questions, the linear inequality may have variables on both sides."] [17592186209881 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice solving absolute value equations. In some cases, we need to first collect like terms that contain absolute value expressions before solving."] [17592186209882 1029 "Questions involving quadratic functions test the understanding of how these functions behave, how to evaluate them, and how to interpret their graphs.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice working with quadratic functions."] [17592186209883 1029 "In machine learning and linear algebra, it’s often convenient to express the Hadamard product of two vectors using matrix multiplication. If $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\odot \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v}) \\mathbf{u} = \\text{diag}(\\mathbf{u}) \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix product\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe notice that the left matrix is diagonal. So, we can write it as a Hadamard product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= diag(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's see a concrete example of expressing a Hadamard product as a matrix product in the next slide."] [17592186209884 1029 "Before we continue, note that the function $\\text{diag}(\\mathbf{v})$ is a **diagonal matrix** with the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb R^n$ on the diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}v_{1} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & v_{2} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & v_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at some concrete examples involving the Hadamard product."] [17592186214144 1029 "This topic will provide practice in analyzing and constructing parallel lines. We'll determine slopes from equations or graphs and write equations for lines parallel to those given."] [17592186214145 1029 "It's essential to understand the meaning of each part of a two-variable linear equation. In this topic, we'll practice interpreting different parts of two-variable models, including constants, variables, and terms."] [17592186214146 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice expressing real-world situations with two-variable linear models."] [17592186214147 1029 "In this lesson, we'll practice constructing tables corresponding to linear relationships, whether shown graphically as lines, written as functions, or expressed as equations. Dealing with functions expressed in tabular form is a common theme of the math section of the SAT exam."] [17592186214148 1029 "In many real-world situations, we want to model a total using two different quantities. When each quantity has a consistent rate or cost, we can write an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + by = c\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ and $y$ represent the two unknown quantities, and $c$ is the total.\n\nFor example, suppose a school buys two types of chairs: folding chairs that cost $\\[math]30$ each and desk chairs that cost $\\[/math]80$ each. Suppose the school spent $\\[math]2,170$ on chairs. How can we model this situation?\n\nLet's break the problem down into explicit steps:\n\n**Step 1**: First, we identify the *unit rates*.\n\n- Each folding chair costs $\\[/math]30$, so the total cost for $f$ folding chairs is\n$30f$.\n- Each desk chair costs $\\[math]80$, so the total cost for $d$ desk chairs is\n$80d$.\n\n**Step 2**: Add the contributions to get the total\n\nThe total cost of all the chairs is the sum of the two parts:\n\n$$\n30f + 80d\n$$\n\nWe are told the school spent $\\[/math]2,170$, so we can write this as\n\n$$\n30f + 80d = 2,170\n$$\n\nThe key takeaways are:\n\n- Multiply each quantity’s *unit rate* by the *number of items.*\n- Add the expressions together.\n- Set the total equal to the given value.\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186214149 1029 "Suppose we have a linear model of the form\n\n$$\nax + by = c\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are unknown quantities, and $c$ is the total.\n\nWe can often solve for one variable if we know the value of the other.\n\nFor example, suppose a theater collected $\\[math]460$ in ticket sales. Adult tickets cost $\\[/math]25$ and child tickets cost $\\[math]10$. If the theater sold $8$ adult tickets, how many child tickets were sold?\n\nThe equation\n\n$$\n25a + 10c = 460\n$$\n\nmodels the total revenue, where $a$ is the number of adult tickets and $c$ is the number of child tickets.\n\nWe are told that $a = 8$. Substituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\n25(8) + 10c = 460\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n200 + 10c = 460\n$$\n\nTo solve for the unknown, we subtract $200$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n10c = 260\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $10{:}$\n\n$$\nc = 26\n$$\n\nTherefore, the theater sold $26$ child tickets.\n\nThe key takeaways for solving for a variable are:\n\n- First, substitute the known value.\n- Then, simplify the equation.\n- Finally, solve for the unknown variable."] [17592186214150 1029 "Recall that the *zero product rule* states that if $ab = 0$, then $a=0$ or $b=0$. A similar rule also works for three or more factors.\n\nIn particular, if\n\n$$\na\\cdot b \\cdot c = 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\na=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad b=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c=0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if we know that the product of several expressions is zero, then at least one of these expressions must be zero.\n\nLet's see how this can be used to solve polynomial equations. For example, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n(x + 12)(x - 10)(x - 11) = 0\n$$\n\nTo solve it, we apply the zero product rule. So, we set each factor equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx + 12 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x - 10 = 0 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad x - 11 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve each of these equations:\n\n- For the first factor:\n$x + 12 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = -12$\n- For the second factor:\n$x - 10 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 10$\n- For the third factor:\n$x - 11 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 11$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = -12,\\ 10,\\ 11\n$$"] [17592186214151 1029 "Let's recall what we learned in a previous lesson about linear equations with infinitely many solutions.\n\nSuppose we have the linear equation\n\n$$\n2x + 5 = 2x + 5\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the coefficient of $x$ on both sides of the equation is $2$, and\n- the constant term on both sides of the equation is $5$.\n\nSince the coefficients of $x$ on both sides of the equation are equal, and the constants are also equal, it follows that this equation has *infinitely many solutions.* In fact, this equation is true for *any* value of $x$.\n\nMore generally, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nIf $a = c$ and $b = d$ are *both* true, then the equation is true for any value of $x$.\n\nThe goal of this lesson is to extend this idea to quadratic polynomials."] [17592186214152 1029 "Suppose we wish to factor the expression\n\n$$\n(x - 4)(x + 1) + (x - 4)(x + 3)\n$$\n\nYou might be tempted to multiply the binomials and then factor the resulting quadratic expression. However, there's a shortcut that makes factoring this expression much easier!\n\nFirst, notice that both terms contain a common factor of $(x-4)$.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}(x - 4)}(x + 1) + {\\color{blue}(x - 4)}(x + 3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can factor out the common factor ${\\color{blue}(x - 4)}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}(x - 4)}\\left[(x + 1) + (x + 3)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets. This gives\n$(x - 4)(2x + 4)$.\n\nAnd we're done! This useful trick is used quite often in higher-level math. The trick is to spot the common factor and factor right away *instead of* multiplying."] [17592186214153 1029 "Suppose we wish to factor the expression\n\n$$\n(8x+3){\\color{blue}{(4-x)}} + {\\color{red}{(x-4)}}(5x-7)\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it seems as though there are no common factors.\n\nHowever, upon closer inspection, we notice that\n\n- the first term contains a factor of ${\\color{blue}{(4-x)}}$, and\n- the second term contains a factor of ${\\color{red}{(x-4)}}$.\n\nThese factors differ only by their sign:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{(x-4)}} = -{\\color{blue}{(4-x)}}\n$$\n\nSo, we first make the factors the same by factoring out $-1$ in the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n(8x+3){\\color{blue}{(4-x)}} - {\\color{blue}{(4-x)}}(5x-7)\n$$\n\nNotice that the plus $(+)$ symbol in front of the second term of our original expression has flipped to a minus symbol $(-)$.\n\nNow that our terms contain a common factor, we can factor the expression. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{(4-x)}}\\left[(8x+3) - (5x-7)\\right]\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the expression inside the square brackets:\n\n$$\n(8x+3) - (5x-7) = 3x + 10\n$$\n\nSo, our fully factored form is\n$(4-x)(3x+10)$."] [17592186214154 1029 "The zero product rule can also be used in reverse. Suppose we would like to find a cubic equation that has solutions\n\n$$\nx = -1, \\qquad x = 3, \\qquad x = 10\n$$\n\nNotice that the given roots can be obtained as solutions to the following linear equations:\n\n- $x + 1 = 0$\n- $x - 3 = 0$\n- $x - 10 = 0$\n\nSo, according to the zero product rule, they are also the solutions of the product\n\n$$\n(x + 1)(x - 3)(x - 10) = 0\n$$\n\nTo find our cubic equation, we expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)(x - 3)(x - 10) &= (x + 1)(x(x - 10) - 3(x - 10)) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)(x^{2} - 10x - 3x + 30) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)(x^{2} - 13x + 30) \\\\\n&= x(x^{2} - 13x + 30) + (x^{2} - 13x + 30) \\\\\n&= x^{3} - 13x^{2} + 30x + x^{2} - 13x + 30 \\\\\n&= x^{3} - 12x^{2} + 17x + 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a cubic equation that has the required solutions is\n\n$$\nx^3 - 12x^2 + 17x + 30 = 0\n$$"] [17592186214155 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to simplify fractions of fractions with algebraic expressions. The core idea is much like simplifying numerical fractions.\n\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{30}{x + 4}\\right)}{15x + 60}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\div (15x + 60)\n$$\n\nNow, we write the divisor as a fraction over $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\div \\dfrac{15x + 60}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{15x + 60}\n$$\n\nNotice that the denominator of the second fraction can be written as\n\n$$\n15x + 60 = 15(x + 4)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we multiply the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{15x + 60} &= \\frac{30}{x + 4} \\cdot (1)/(15(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (30 \\cdot 1)/((x + 4) \\cdot 15(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (30)/(15(x + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify by canceling out common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(30)/(15(x + 4)^{2}) &= (2 \\cdot 15)/(15(x + 4)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2)/((x + 4)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{30}{x + 4}\\right)}{15x + 60} = \\dfrac{2}{(x + 4)^2}\n$$\n\nvalid for all\n\n$$\nx\\neq -4\n$$"] [17592186214156 1029 "Now, suppose we wish to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{6}{x - 7} + x + 5}\n$$\n\nTo simplify this expression using the techniques we've discussed so far, we need to express the numerator as a rational expression.\n\nFirst, we consider the denominator only:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{x - 7} + x + 5\n$$\n\nWe can express the polynomial part as a rational expression with a denominator of $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{x - 7} + \\dfrac{x + 5}{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we form a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second term by $(x-7){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{x - 7} + \\frac{x + 5}{1} &= \\frac{6}{x - 7} + \\frac{x - 7}{x - 7} \\cdot \\frac{x + 5}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{x - 7} + ((x - 7)(x + 5))/(x - 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add our expressions as usual.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6}{x - 7} + ((x - 7)(x + 5))/(x - 7) &= (6 + (x - 7)(x + 5))/(x - 7) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 + x^{2} - 2x - 35}{x - 7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} - 2x - 29}{x - 7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNtext, we rewrite our original expression as a fraction of fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{6}{x - 7} + x + 5} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2 - 2x - 29}{x - 7}\\right)}\n$$\n\nWriting the fraction as a division, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2 - 2x - 29}{x - 7}\\right)} = 1 \\div \\dfrac{x^2 - 2x - 29}{x - 7}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that division is equivalent to multiplication by the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 \\div \\frac{x^{2} - 2x - 29}{x - 7} &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{x - 7}{x^{2} - 2x - 29} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x - 7}{x^{2} - 2x - 29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider some more examples."] [17592186214157 1029 "We can add two rational expressions by expressing each fraction using a common denominator before performing the addition. In this lesson, we'll discuss how factoring the denominators first can make this process easier.\n\nLet's consider the following addition problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3+6x}{x^2+xy}+\\dfrac{1-y}{y+x}\n$$\n\nWe can write these two over a common denominator by\n\n- multiplying the numerator and denominator of the first fraction by $y+x$, and\n- multiplying the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $x^2+xy$.\n\nThis will lead to the common denominator $(y+x)(x^2+xy){:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y+x}{y+x} \\cdot \\dfrac{3+6x}{x^2+xy} + \\dfrac{x^2+xy}{x^2+xy} \\cdot \\dfrac{1-y}{y+x}\n$$\n\nThis is a fairly complex-looking expression that will take some effort to simplify.\n\nHowever, in this case, it turns out that we can simplify the computations by forming a simpler common denominator. Let's see how."] [17592186214158 1029 "We can use the distributive laws\n\n$$\n(a+b) \\cdot c = a \\cdot c+b \\cdot c\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\na\\cdot (b+c) = a \\cdot b+a \\cdot c\n$$\n\nto simplify exponential expressions.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to simplify the expression\n\n$$\n\\left(3d^2 e^{5} f + 6e f^3 \\right) f^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we apply the distributive law.\n\n$$\n3d^2 e^{5} f \\cdot f^2 + 6e f^3 \\cdot f^2\n$$\n\nNext, we combine exponents with like bases. In this case, both terms have multiple factors containing powers of $f$, so let's group these together using parentheses:\n\n$$\n3d^2 e^{5} \\left(f \\cdot f^2 \\right) + 6e \\left(f^3 \\cdot f^2 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the laws of exponents. In this case, we apply the product rule for exponents:\n$3d^{2}e^{5}f^{1 + 2} + 6ef^{3 + 2}$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n3d^2 e^{5} f^3 + 6e f^5\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186214159 1029 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply the distributive law to exponential expressions containing one or more variables. In this lesson, we'll see how the same techniques can be used for expanding radical expressions.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to expand the parentheses in the following expression.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{y}\\left(\\sqrt{y} + \\sqrt{yz}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can rewrite the radicals in our expression using fractional exponents and then apply the distributive law and the laws of exponents.\n\nFirst, we write radicals as fractional exponents:\n\n$$\ny^{1/2}(y^{1/2} + (yz)^{1/2})\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the power of product rule to distribute the second exponent inside the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\ny^{1/2}(y^{1/2} + y^{1/2}z^{1/2})\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\ny^{1/2}\\cdot y^{1/2} + y^{1/2}\\cdot y^{1/2}z^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule for exponents to combine the powers of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{1/2} \\cdot 3y^{1/2} + y^{1/2} \\cdot y^{1/2}z^{1/2} &= (y^{1/2} \\cdot y^{1/2}) + (y^{1/2} \\cdot y^{1/2})z^{1/2} \\\\\n&= y^{1} + y^{1}z^{1/2} \\\\\n&= y + yz^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original expression contained radicals, we write our final expression using radicals, too. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\ny + yz^{1/2} = y + y\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\nWe can apply similar techniques to expressions containing fractions. Let's see an example."] [17592186214160 1029 "We sometimes encounter situations where common factors in the denominator are somewhat obscured. A common example is when one factor differs from another by sign only. Here, we'll learn how to deal with this kind of problem.\n\nFor example, consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}1-x}-\\dfrac{2}{x^2-x}\n$$\n\nWe start by factoring the polynomial in the second denominator, which yields\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}1-x}-\\dfrac{2}{x(x-1)}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The denominator of the first term is ${\\color{blue}{1-x}}$.\n- The second factor in the denominator of the second term is $x - 1$, which differs from the denominator of the first term by a factor of $({\\color{red}{-1}}){:}$\n\n$$\nx-1 = ({\\color{red}{-1}})({\\color{blue}1-x})\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our subtraction problem by introducing a negative sign in the denominator of the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{1 - x} - (2)/(x(x - 1)) &= \\frac{5}{1 - x} - (2)/(x(-1)(1 - x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{1 - x} - (2)/(-x(1 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get rid of the minus sign in the second denominator, we multiply the numerator and denominator of the second fraction by $-1$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{1 - x} - (2)/(-x(1 - x)) &= \\frac{5}{1 - x} - ((-1) \\cdot 2)/((-1) \\cdot (-x(1 - x))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{1 - x} - (-1) \\cdot (2)/(x(1 - x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{1 - x} + (2)/(x(1 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis shows that we can pull out the minus sign from the denominator (or numerator) of a fraction and write it before the fraction. Notice that this has turned our subtraction problem into one involving addition.\n\nNow, we can put each rational expression over a common denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the first fraction by $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{1 - x} + (2)/(x(1 - x)) &= \\frac{x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{5}{1 - x} + (2)/(x(1 - x)) \\\\\n&= (5x)/(x(1 - x)) + (2)/(x(1 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the rational expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5x)/(x(1 - x)) + (2)/(x(1 - x)) &= (5x + 2)/(x(1 - x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186214161 1029 "Suppose we have two quadratic polynomials $P$ and $Q$, given by\n\n$$\nP(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Q(x) = dx^2 + ex + f\n$$\n\nIf these polynomials are equal for *all* values of $x$, we say that they're **identical.** This means their matching terms must be the same. In other words, we must have\n\n$$\na = d, \\qquad b = e, \\qquad c = f\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b$, and $c$ are fixed numbers, and we're told that this equation is true for all values of $x$.\n\nSince this polynomial equation is true for all values of $x$, we can match the coefficients.\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a$, and on the right-hand side it is $1$.\nSo, we have\n$a = 1$.\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b$, and on the right-hand side it is $5$.\nSo, we have\n$b = 5$.\n- The constant on the left-hand side is $c$, and on the right-hand side it is $4$.\nSo, we have\n$c = 4$.\n\nThis process is known as **equating coefficients.** It’s a useful tool whenever we know two polynomials are equal for all values of the variable.\n\nThis idea works for higher-degree polynomials too: when two polynomials are equal for all $x$, we can compare the coefficients of each power of $x$ and set them equal to each other."] [17592186214162 1029 "In this lesson, we'll explore how to find the area of an arbitrary triangle in the coordinate plane.\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with vertices\n\n$$\nA(2,3), \\qquad B(6,7), \\qquad C(8,2)\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6178/Source/Images/281974827fbbbb46a0f872e1050dd93d.png)\n\nFinding the area of this triangle using the usual methods is tricky because it's not easy to determine the length of the perpendicular height to a given base. So instead, we will proceed using an indirect method.\n\nFirst, we notice that we can **inscribe** our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6178/Source/Images/fa7e4ab3f6de8bb845f934f73c6e3eb0.png)\n\nInscribing a triangle in a rectangle means drawing a rectangle around the triangle in such a way that the triangle lies completely inside the rectangle and each of the triangles vertices touches the rectangle.\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area that we denote by $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area that we denote by $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area that we denote by $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nIn other words, the area of the required (blue) triangle equals the area of the orange rectangle minus the areas of the three triangles with areas $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$. Notice that it's much easier to find the areas of these three triangles compared to the blue triangle, since their bases and heights are easily determined from the diagram.\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 6 \\cdot 5; = 30$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4; = 8$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5; = 5$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1; = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 30 - 8 - 5 - 3 = 14\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [17592186214163 1029 "Let's consider our rational expression once more.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3+6x}{x^2+xy}+\\dfrac{1-y}{y+x}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can factor the polynomial in the first denominator as\n\n$$\nx^2+xy = x(x+y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, can rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3+6x}{x(x+y)}+\\dfrac{1-y}{x+y}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n- the denominator of the first term is $x(x+y)$,\n- the denominator of the second term is $(x+y)$, and\n- the least common multiple of the two denominators is $x(x+y)$.\n\nTherefore, we can put these two expressions over a common denominator of $x(x+y)$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator of the *second* term by ${\\color{blue}{x}}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 + 6x)/(x(x + y)) + \\frac{1 - y}{x + y} &= (3 + 6x)/(x(x + y)) + \\frac{x}{x} \\cdot \\frac{1 - y}{x + y} \\\\\n&= (3 + 6x)/(x(x + y)) + (x(1 - y))/(x(x + y))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we obtained a much simpler expression than we did previously!\n\nFinally, we add our expressions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 + 6x)/(x(x + y)) + (x(1 - y))/(x(x + y)) &= (3 + 6x + x(1 - y))/(x(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (3 + 6x + x - xy)/(x(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (3 + 7x - xy)/(x(x + y))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186214164 1029 "Recall that in right triangles, trigonometric ratios such as sine and cosine describe the relationships between the sides and angles.\nIf we know one trigonometric ratio, we can often use it to determine other ratios.\n\nFor example, consider $\\triangle ABC$, where $\\angle B$ is the right angle, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6175/Source/Images/439437702104deb8c494bd5a64b450bc.png)\n\nSuppose we are told that\n\n$$\n\\sin A = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\cos C$?\n\nRecall that in such a triangle,\n\n- the sine of the acute angle $\\angle A$ is given by\n$\\sin A = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite to A}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$,\n- the cosine of the acute angle $\\angle C$ is given by\n$\\cos C = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent to C}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$.\n\nNotice that the leg that is adjacent to $\\angle C$ is the same as the leg that is opposite to $\\angle A{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{adjacent to C}} = {\\color{blue}\\text{opposite to A}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos C &= \\frac{adjacent to C}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n&= \\frac{opposite to A}{hypotenuse} \\\\\n&= \\sin A \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186214165 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to derive the equation of a translated function given the equation of some other function.\n\nFor example, consider a line defined by\n\n$$\ny = \\underbrace{-x + 2}_{\\large f(x)}\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the line obtained by translating this line by $6$ units up.\n\nFor each point on the original line $y = f(x)$, we must add $6$ to each $y$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new line. So, the $y$-coordinates of the points on the new line are given by\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{new}} = y + 6\n$$\n\nWe introduce the \"$\\textrm{new}$\" suffix to distinguish $y$-values on the translated line from those on the original line.\n\nSubstituting the equation of our original line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 2\n$$\n\ninto the above equation gives\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{new}} = (-x + 2) + 6\n$$\n\nSince our equation no longer refers to points on the original line, we can drop the \"$\\textrm{new}$\" suffix, which gives\n\n$$\ny = (-x + 2) + 6\n$$\n\nFinally, collecting the like terms, we have\n\n$$\ny = -x + 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that if we translate the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 2\n$$\n\nby $6$ units *up*, we get the line\n\n$$\ny = -x + 8\n$$\n\nWe can apply the same method to translate a quadratic function. Let's see how."] [17592186214166 1029 "Let's now see how to deal with horizontal translations.\n\nFor example, suppose a line is defined by\n\n$$\ny = \\underbrace{-2x + 3}_{\\large f(x)}\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the line we get when we translate this line by $2$ units *left.*\n\nFor each point on the line $y = f(x)$, we must subtract $2$ from each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new line. So, the $x$-coordinates of the points on the new line are given by\n\n$$\nx_{\\textrm{new}} = x - 2\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\nx = x_{\\textrm{new}} + 2\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of the new line, we substitute this into the equation of the first line. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2x + 3 \\\\\ny &= -2(x_{new} + 2) + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\ny = -2(x + 2) + 3\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying this expression using the distributive law and collecting like terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2x - 4 + 3 \\\\\ny &= -2x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186214167 1029 "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$5y|= 32; \\frac{5y}{5}|= \\frac{32}{5}; y|= \\frac{32}{5}$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$5y - 6|= 5 \\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}) - 6; = 32 - 6; = 26$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$5y - 6|= (32) - 6; = 26$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186214168 1029 "In many real-world scenarios, quantities can have multiple constraints at once. We can often model such situations using *compound inequalities*.\n\nFor example, suppose the cost of a movie ticket is $\\[math]12$ per person, and during the weekend, between $100$ and $150$ people attend the movie screenings each day. We can use this information to find a compound inequality that must be satisfied by the cinema's daily earnings.\n\nSince the number of attendees ranges from $100$ to $150$ per day, we can calculate the range of possible earnings:\n\n- If $100$ people attend per day, and the ticket price is $\\[/math]12$, then the minimum earnings are given by\n$\\[math]12 \\cdot 100 = \\[/math]1\\,200$.\n- If $150$ people attend per day, and the ticket price is $\\[math]12$, then the maximum earnings are given by\n$\\[/math]12 \\cdot 150 = \\[math]1\\,800$.\n\nTherefore, if $P$ represents the daily earnings of the cinema, in dollars, we have\n\n$$\n1\\,200 \\leq P \\leq 1\\,800\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186214169 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine trigonometric ratios of large angles in degrees.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(7,230^\\circ\\right)\n$$\n\nTrigonometric ratios like sine and cosine repeat every $360^\\circ$ (full turns). So, instead of evaluating a large angle directly, we reduce it to a smaller, coterminal angle between $0^\\circ$ and\n$360^\\circ$.\n\nTo do this, we first find an angle that's coterminal with $7,230^\\circ$ and lies in the range $[MATH: [0,360^\\circ).]$\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $360^\\circ$. So, we divide $7,230$ by $360$ with remainder:\n\n$$\n7,230 \\div 360 = 20 \\ \\text{R} \\ 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $7,230$ as a multiple if $360$ *plus* our remainder as follows:\n\n$$\n7,230 = 20 \\cdot 360 + 30\n$$\n\nAdding in our degree symbol, we have\n\n$$\n7, 230^{∘} = 20 \\cdot 360^{∘} + 30^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, we can reduce our large angle of $7,230^\\circ$ by $20$ full turns of $360^\\circ$, since adding or subtracting a full turn doesn't change the sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(7,230^\\circ\\right) = \\sin\\left({\\color{blue}20 \\cdot 360^\\circ} + 30^\\circ\\right) = \\sin\\left(30^\\circ\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6179/Source/Images/33622094d22c5e6d891e89ba031f2da9.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $30^\\circ$ corresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(30^\\circ\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(7,230^\\circ\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186214170 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine trigonometric ratios of large angles in radians.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{203\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nTrigonometric ratios like sine and cosine repeat every $2\\pi$ radians (full turns). So, instead of evaluating a large angle directly, we reduce it to a smaller, coterminal angle between $0$ and\n$2\\pi$. To do this, we write the angle as a sum of multiples of $2\\pi$ plus a remainder.\n\nWe first find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{203\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[MATH: [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\nSo, we divide $203$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n203 \\div 8 = 25 \\ \\text{R} \\ 3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n203 = 25 \\cdot 8 + 3\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{203π}{4} &= ((25 \\cdot 8 + 3)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{3π}{4} \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{3π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can reduce our large angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{203\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nby $25$ full turns of $2\\pi$, as adding or subtracting a full turn doesn't change the sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{203\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sin \\left({\\color{blue}25 \\cdot 2\\pi} + \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6184/Source/Images/fe5847cb99ec31312e22032a3860e6bc.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{203\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}2\n$$"] [17592186214171 1029 "We often use linear functions in real-world situations to describe relationships between quantities.\n\nFor example, suppose a writer is tracking their progress while typing a draft. They observe that\n\n- after $2$ minutes, they have typed $300$ words, and\n- after $5$ minutes, they have typed $750$ words.\n\nWe can build a linear function to model this situation.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of minutes, and $g(x)$ represent the number of words typed after $x$ minutes.\nThen, the linear function modeling this situation is\n\n$$\ng(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nfor some slope $m$ and value $b$, which we need to find.\n\nFrom the two observations, we know two points on the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{1}, y_{1}) &= (2, 300) \\\\\n(x_{2}, y_{2}) &= (5, 750)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the given two points, we find the slope $m$ using the slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{750 - 300}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{450}{3} \\\\\n&= 150\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the writer is typing at a rate of $150$ words per minute.\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&= 150x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we substitute one of the points, say $(2, 300)$, into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n300 &= 150 \\cdot 2 + b \\\\\n300 &= 300 + b \\\\\nb &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means the starting number of words is $0$ - the writer began typing from scratch.\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = 150x\n$$"] [17592186214172 1029 "Sometimes, when modeling real-life situations with linear functions, the independent variable does not start at zero.\n\nFor example, suppose a puzzle enthusiast is working on a large jigsaw puzzle. Their progress follows this pattern:\n\n- On the first day, they place $50$ pieces.\n- They add $30$ more pieces on each of the following days.\n\nWith this, we can construct a linear function $P$ for the total number of puzzle pieces placed after $d$ days, where\n\n$$\nd \\geq 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's break down the pattern to understand it:\n\n- On day $d=1$, they place $P=50$ pieces.\n- On day $d=2$, they place $P=50+30=80$ pieces in total.\n- On day $d=3$, they place $P=50+30+30=110$ pieces in total.\n- $\\cdots$\n\nAnd so on.\n\nNotice that the amount added each day after the first is always $30$. So the total number of pieces placed includes\n\n- the initial $50$ pieces placed on the first day, and\n- $30$ pieces for each additional day *beyond the first*. Since the first $50$ pieces are placed on day $1$, the number of *additional* pieces placed by day $d$ equals\n$30(d - 1)$.\n\nTherefore, the total number of pieces $P$ placed after $d$ days, where\n\n$$\nd \\geq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(d) &= 50 + 30(d - 1) \\\\\n&= 50 + 30d - 30 \\\\\n&= 30d + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186214173 1029 "Rationalizing the denominator works similarly for algebraic expressions as it does for numerical ones. When the denominator contains two terms and includes radicals, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the *conjugate* of the denominator to eliminate the radical.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{x}$, all we have to do is flip the sign between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is\n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $1-\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{x}}{1 - \\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{x})/((1 + \\sqrt{x})(1 - \\sqrt{x})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{x})/(1^{2} - (\\sqrt{x})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{x}}{1 - x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186214174 1029 "Let's consider the same data set once more, but this time we'll use cumulative frequency to calculate the median.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ |\n| $10$ | $1$ |\n| $13$ | $1$ |\n\nWe first need to construct the corresponding *cumulative frequency* table. So, let's start by adding another column to store our cumulative frequencies.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$ |  |\n| $7$ | $1$ |  |\n| $10$ | $1$ |  |\n| $13$ | $1$ |  |\n\nRecall that the cumulative frequency column shows the running total of the frequencies:\n\n- For the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n- For each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative total from the previous row.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add the missing values in the cumulative frequency table.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| $10$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $13$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values in the dataset is $7$ (the final cumulative frequency). Since our dataset has an odd number of data points, the median is the middle value in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMedian position &= \\frac{7 + 1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{2} \\\\\n&= 4th\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let’s determine where the $4$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see that the first row of the table covers the first $4$ positions in total.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $7$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| $10$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $13$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\nSo, the $4$th position falls into the first row that corresponds to the value $3$. Therefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\text{Median} = 3\n$$\n\nIn this example, the position we wanted (the $4$th position) coincided with a value in the cumulative frequency column. When this doesn't happen, we need to think a little more carefully about which value in the table to select as the median.\n\nLet's see an example."] [17592186214175 1029 "Recall that the *mode* of a data set is the value that occurs most often in a data set. In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the mode of a data set presented as a frequency table.\n\nConsider a data set with the corresponding frequency table shown below. What is the mode of this data set?\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $7$ |\n| $12$ | $3$ |\n| $16$ | $4$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the highest frequency is $7$, corresponding to the value $8$.\n\n| Value | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $5$ | $2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}8$ | $\\color{blue}7$ |\n| $12$ | $3$ |\n| $16$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the mode of the data set is $8$.\n\nRecall that some data sets have more than one mode. Let's see an example of how this looks when analyzing frequency tables."] [17592186214176 1029 "When *grouped* data is displayed in a histogram, we can only *estimate* the median. This is because we don't know the exact values that make up our data set.\n\nTo estimate the median in such cases, we create a cumulative frequency table as before, and then find the *group* in which the median lies.\n\nLet’s see how this works with an example. In which group does the median of the following dataset lie?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6190/Source/Images/c70ecb5823a5325b265bbed877bee015.png)\n\nThe cumulative frequency table corresponding to this histogram is as follows:\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $9-12$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $12-15$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $15-18$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $18-21$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| $21-24$ | $5$ | $17$ |\n\nThus, the total number of values is\n\n$$\n1+2+3+6+5=17\n$$\n\nSince $17$ is an odd number, the median is in the following position:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{17+1}{2}=\\dfrac{18}{2}=9\\textrm{th}\n$$\n\nNow we determine where the $9$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in our table, we see the following:\n\n- Up to the third row, the table covers $6$ positions in total.\n- Up to the fourth row, the table covers $12$ positions in total.\n\nTherefore, the $9$th value falls in the fourth interval, which is\n$18-21$.\n\nSo, the true median lies somewhere between $18$ and $21$."] [17592186214177 1029 "We often want a single number that gives us a sense of the \"typical\" or \"average\" value in a data set. This is where measures of central tendency, such as the mean, become especially useful.\n\nThe mean summarizes all the values in a data set into a *single number* by balancing them out. This makes the mean a powerful tool for comparing two groups.\n\nFor example, suppose a study compared students' reading proficiencies in two 4th-grade classes. Both classes sat the same standardized test. The students in class A achieved a mean score of $8.0$. The results of the students in class B are given below:\n\n$$\n6,\\quad 5,\\quad 8,\\quad 10,\\quad 10,\\quad 4,\\quad 6\n$$\n\nBy comparing the mean scores of classes A and B, what can we say about the relative performance of the two classes on this test?\n\nLet's start by computing the mean of class B's results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Mean}(B) = \\dfrac{6+5+8+10+10+4+6}{7} = 7.0\n$$\n\nSo, class A's mean score is higher, which *suggests* that class A performed better on the test overall.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- It's crucial to recognize that there are various methods for comparing two data sets. Comparing the means is only one way.\n- Examining multiple measures together provides a more comprehensive view of the data.\n- So in the example above, while comparing the means might *suggest* that class A performed better, we would need to gather more evidence—by considering the median, mode, and the overall spread of scores—to draw a fairer conclusion."] [17592186214178 1029 "We often want a single number that gives us a sense of the \"typical\" or \"average\" value in a data set. This is where measures of central tendency, such as the median, become especially useful.\n\nThe median identifies the *middle value* in a data set when the numbers are arranged in order. This makes the median a powerful tool for comparing two groups, especially when extreme values (outliers) might distort the mean.\n\nFor example, suppose a nutrition study compared the number of servings of fruits and vegetables eaten per week by participants in two different communities. Let's assume we know that participants in community A had a median of $7.0$ servings per week, and the results for participants in community B are as follows:\n\n$$\n4,\\quad 5,\\quad 6,\\quad 6,\\quad 8,\\quad 10,\\quad 10\n$$\n\nBy comparing the median values of communities A and B, what can we say about their relative fruit and vegetable consumption?\n\nLet's start by computing the median of community B's results. Since there are $7$ data points, the median is the middle value:\n\n$4,\\quad 5,\\quad 6,\\quad {\\color{blue}6},\\quad 8,\\quad 10,\\quad 10$\nTherefore, the median of community B is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Median}(B) = 6\n$$\n\nSince $7 > 6$, community A's median is higher, which *suggests* that participants in community A consumed more fruits and vegetables overall.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- It's crucial to recognize that there are various methods for comparing two data sets. Comparing the medians is only one way.\n- Examining multiple measures together provides a more comprehensive view of the data.\n- In the example above, while comparing the medians might *suggest* that community A consumed more, we would need to gather more evidence—by considering the mean, mode, and the overall spread of values—to draw a fairer conclusion."] [17592186214179 1029 "Now, suppose we have the following line, given in standard form:\n\n$$\nx + y = -4\n$$\n\nSuppose we translate this line by $2$ units up. We can find the equation of the new line using the same technique as before!\n\nFor each point on the line, we must add $2$ to each $y$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on the new line. So, the $y$-coordinates of the points on the new line are given by\n\n$$\ny_{\\textrm{new}} = y + 2\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny = y_{\\textrm{new}} - 2\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of the new line, we substitute this into the equation of the first line. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= -4 \\\\\nx + (y_{new} - 2) &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\nx + (y - 2) = -4\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing this equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + (y - 2) &= -4 \\\\\nx + y - 2 &= -4 \\\\\nx + y &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider a situation where we must perform a horizontal translation of a line given in standard form."] [17592186214180 1029 "Suppose that\n\n$$\na,b,c,d \\neq 0\n$$\n\nThen, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}a}{\\color{red}b} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}c}{\\color{red}d}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the equation obtained by taking the reciprocal of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}b}{\\color{blue}a} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}d}{\\color{blue}c}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{w}{2} = 3\n$$\n\nProvided that\n\n$$\nw\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can determine the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac2w\n$$\n\n*without* solving for $w$ first.\n\nFirst, we express the right-hand side as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{w}{2} &= \\frac{3}{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal of both sides, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{w} &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the the condition\n\n$$\nw\\neq 0\n$$\n\nis important. If $w = 0$ then the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac2w\n$$\n\nis undefined!"] [17592186214181 1029 "A **cumulative frequency table** is an extension of a regular frequency table that shows how frequencies *accumulate* across an *ordered data set*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's fill in the missing values in the following cumulative frequency table.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |\n| $3$ | $1$ |  |\n| $4$ | $7$ |  |\n\nThe cumulative frequency column shows the *running total* of the frequencies.\n\nTo complete the cumulative frequency column, we start with the first value. Then, we walk down the table, adding each new frequency to the cumulative frequency in the previous row.\n\nFor the first row, the cumulative frequency is simply equal to the frequency of that row.\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | ${\\color{blue}3}$ | ${\\color{blue}3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |\n| $3$ | $1$ |  |\n| $4$ | $7$ |  |\n\nFor each subsequent row, the cumulative frequency is found by adding the frequency of that row to the cumulative frequency *from the previous row*.\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $2$nd row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}+{\\color{red}3}=5\n$$\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | ${\\color{red}3}$ |\n| $2$ | ${\\color{blue}2}$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ |  |\n| $4$ | $7$ |  |\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $3$rd row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{red}5}=6\n$$\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | ${\\color{red}5}$ |\n| $3$ | ${\\color{blue}1}$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ |  |\n\nThe cumulative frequency for the $4$th row equals\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{red}6} = 13\n$$\n\n| Value | Frequency | Cumulative Frequency |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | ${\\color{red}6}$ |\n| $4$ | ${\\color{blue}7}$ | $13$ |\n\nThis gives us our completed cumulative frequency table."] [17592186214182 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the mean, median, and mode from a histogram.\n\nLet's begin with the mode. For example, what is the mode of the dataset represented by the histogram below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6190/Source/Images/b5d89594c394d265ddb81e63e68e80e8.png)\n\nThe mode is the value or values that appear most frequently in a dataset.\n\nFrom a histogram, we can directly compare the heights of each bar to see how often each value occurs.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6190/Source/Images/105e14d9f79f6fe46313f25b58478098.png)\n\nIn this case, the tallest bars correspond to the values $18$ and $21$, which both appear in the dataset $8$ times. Since both of these values occur most frequently and with equal frequency, the dataset is **bimodal** (i.e., it has two modes). Therefore, the modes are $18$ and $21$.\n\nLet's now examine an example involving grouped data."]}
